<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC
  "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN"
  "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>
<overview>
<p>
A derivation declares both <code>meson</code> and <code>cmake</code> in
<code>nativeBuildInputs</code>. In nixpkgs, these are alternative
configure-phase build systems and most packages should pick one.
</p>
<p>
When both are present, the Meson setup usually wins and the CMake setup is
skipped, so the extra tool only increases the closure and can mislead readers
into thinking both build systems are required.
</p>
<p>
There are exceptions: mixed-build-system subprojects, or Meson dependencies
that can only be located through CMake's <code>find_package</code> fallback.
If one of those applies, keep both tools but add a nearby comment explaining
why.
</p>
</overview>

<recommendation>
<p>
Remove the unused tool from <code>nativeBuildInputs</code>. Usually that means
keeping only <code>meson</code> for a Meson package, or only <code>cmake</code>
for a CMake package. If both are genuinely required, document the exception in
code.
</p>
</recommendation>

<example>
<p>Problematic — both build systems are declared:</p>
<sample language="nix">
{ stdenv, meson, cmake }:
stdenv.mkDerivation {
  pname = "demo";
  version = "1.0";
  nativeBuildInputs = [ meson cmake ];
}
</sample>
<p>Recommended — keep only the primary build system:</p>
<sample language="nix">
{ stdenv, meson }:
stdenv.mkDerivation {
  pname = "demo";
  version = "1.0";
  nativeBuildInputs = [ meson ];
}
</sample>
</example>

<references>
<li>
<a href="https://github.com/jtojnar/nixpkgs-hammering/blob/main/explanations/meson-cmake.md">
<code>nixpkgs-hammering</code> explanation: <code>meson-cmake</code></a>
</li>
<li>
Meson documentation:
<a href="https://mesonbuild.com/CMake-module.html#cmake-subprojects">CMake subprojects</a>,
<a href="https://mesonbuild.com/Release-notes-for-0-49-0.html#cmake-find_package-dependency-backend">CMake <code>find_package</code> fallback</a>
</li>
</references>
</qhelp>
